    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's political when you start looking at it, politically speaking, that you can't have some people say, well, you should have Israel vote by itself, Ukraine's vote by itself, Indo-Pacific, Taiwan by itself, and the border by itself. There's not enough votes to get any of that by itself, because there's a group of people that will not support Democrats' border security. There's a group of people that will not support Ukraine, mainly Republicans. There's a group of people that will not support Israel, mainly Democrats. In fact, Republicans don't know any by this vote against that. And then Indo-Pacific is like, but it's all important because it's all about our national security posture. and international security posture as well. You know, there's this movement among Republicans this point that's actually pushing itself to become an isolationist country. Why are we involved in foreign affairs? Why are we doing this? Why are we doing that? Because somebody has to. And if the United States isn't there, then the gap is only going to be filled either by a communist country in China or a dictator country, communist country in Russia. led by President Xi or led by Putin. When you control the world and you're the world, please, you also control the economy. If you control the economy, Do you think the dollar will still be the number one currency in the world that will set the standard? Or do you think maybe the yen or the ruble might take us place? Well, if you're the dominant country, your currency is going to probably be it. And then they slowly allow us to be an isolation country. They push us into a weaker and weaker state. They have no intentions of invading us until their point gets to so weak that we can't make it by ourselves, but they can push us and make it so irrelevant. And every time we waver in support of a country that is an ally with us, because it is our role, we are there to assist. That's why people are allies with us. They're not all allies with us just because we're good people. Some people just don't like redheads. But they like you because, well, because they do business with you. And I'm certain that's just the way that works. Some people don't like us because we're Americans and we think we're better than everybody else. But they, they, they want to do business with us because economically it's important for them to do business with us. So it all has a give and take. At the end of the day, we have to be there or someone else is going to fill that vacuum. And if we waver on our allies, like if we waver on Israel, It sends bag signals. If we waver on Ukraine, when we asked them to give up their nuclear weapons in 1994 and said, if you do so, we will be there in the event that you get invaded by Russia specifically. And we signed the Budapest memorandum tell them that. And we waver on that, then that means the word of the United States isn't any good. And what has always been the most powerful thing about the United States as everybody could trust what we said. If we said something, we were going to do it. Other countries wasn't able to fulfill that promise. We have historically have always been able to fulfill that promise. And now we're wavering on a word, and that is a huge red flag around the world, which is why you see him president Xi be so aggressive with his one belt and one road initiative, because he sees where America's going, and he's preparing himself to fill that void because China wants to be the world power.",
